380 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (An infrared \(IR\) receiver is an electronic component designed to detect infrared light signals, usually in the wavelength range of 850 nm to 950 nm, and convert them into electrical signals that can be processed by a circuit. It is often used in conjunction with an IR transmitter for wireless communication, such as in TV remotes, air conditioner controls, and other remote-operated devices. Most IR receivers include a photodiode or phototransistor to sense the IR light, along with built-in signal processing circuitry that filters and demodulates the incoming signal, typically modulated at around 38 kHz to avoid interference from ambient light sources. The output is usually a digital signal that changes state when the correct IR pulses are detected. IR receivers are compact, inexpensive, and reliable for short-range, line-of-sight communication. In circuit diagrams, they are represented as a photodiode or a small sensor package with arrows pointing inward, indicating incoming light )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
